The project contains original source code, build scripts and documentation.
It depends on third-party software, including Zakuro and 3dsrecomp (MIT), the
idevice crate used by tools/devpush (MIT, https://github.com/jkcoxson/idevice,
pinned commit d32c8189c51c2789496b0768039419c3705498c3, v0.1.68), and S2Arm
(Apache-2.0) as the API-usage reference for the device bridge. See
THIRD_PARTY.md for the full list and licenses.

The Windows launcher bundles the official Impactor v2.7.3 and plumesign release
files (unmodified) so the user can sign the base shell IPA with their own Apple ID;
the macOS launcher signs through Xcode and does not bundle Impactor.
Impactor is MIT (Copyright 2025 Samara M) and embeds apple-codesign 0.29.0 under
MPL-2.0. The binaries are downloaded from the official GitHub release
(https://github.com/claration/impactor/releases/tag/v2.7.3) at launcher packaging
time, verified against pinned SHA-256 values, and are not committed to this
repository; the MIT and MPL-2.0 license texts and source links are kept in
licenses/impactor/. 3ds2apple does not implement Apple ID authentication
and never receives or logs the user's password or session tokens. plumesign is the
command-line signer from the same official Impactor v2.7.3 release; it is shipped
unmodified under the same upstream MIT licence and its SHA-256 is pinned as well.

The iOS shell template and the macOS launcher bundle the unmodified MoltenVK 1.4.2
framework (Apache-2.0, https://github.com/KhronosGroup/MoltenVK) for the
Vulkan-to-Metal renderer path. The framework is a build/packaging input and is not
committed to this repository; its licence is listed in THIRD_PARTY.md, and the
project's Apache-2.0 LICENSE (the same licence text MoltenVK uses) is included in
every release package from 0.1.1 on. See "License texts in the release packages"
in THIRD_PARTY.md.

The Windows single-file launcher is a self-contained .NET 10 application (MIT) using
the Avalonia 12.1.6 UI framework (MIT), built only from the sources in this
repository. The launcher embeds the project LICENSE/NOTICE/THIRD_PARTY texts and the
relevant third-party licence files in its payload and extracts them on first run; the
release zip also carries the three project texts at its root.

The device bridge does not contain or distribute game data. Game files are
pushed by the user from their own machine to their own device; ROMs, IPAs and
generated C code stay out of git.
The Windows/Linux local-build path (tools/winbuild, the Windows launcher's only
GamePack build path since 0.1.0) uses the official open-source LLVM/Clang/LLD
23.1.6 release, licensed under the Apache License v2.0 WITH LLVM-exception
(https://llvm.org/LICENSE.txt). No Apple
SDK, iPhoneOS stub library or Xcode component is used, copied or redistributed by
that path; it links the GamePack with -undefined dynamic_lookup so libSystem and
the app's runtime resolve host symbols at dlopen() time. The LLVM release files are
not committed to this repository: the launcher bundles an official LLVM build
unmodified, verifies it against the SHA-256 values pinned in
tools/winbuild/llvm-manifest.json, and the LLVM licence text plus copyright
notices are shipped alongside them (source: https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project).
3dsrecomp declares MIT in its Cargo.toml but ships no LICENSE file upstream; it is
only run as a build tool (cloned at a pinned revision at build time) and is never
vendored or redistributed by this project.

Public binaries are distributed only through the project's GitHub Releases
(https://github.com/HFYKratos/3ds2apple/releases): each release publishes exactly the
Windows and macOS launcher zips. From 0.1.1 on, each zip contains LICENSE, NOTICE and
THIRD_PARTY.md at its root, byte-identical to the copies in this repository, so the
licence texts ship with the binaries and are not separate release assets; the ROM-free
shell template is embedded in the launchers. GPL projects are read-only references and
contribute no code or text to the packages. No Nintendo ROMs, decryption keys,
BIOS or firmware, generated C, per-game GamePack dylibs or composed game IPAs are
published, and the release policy/checks are documented in
CONTRIBUTING.md#release-policy.
